But
the developer (*ahem* ... me) does offer the source code to anyone who
would like to build in another platform.  Unfortunatly, due to the
nature of
the beast, it would be a difficult port, but I could also offer my
sympathy :-)

It really wasn't that difficult to put together, once the central
concept had been
worked out.   I started by making a screen which addressed a graphic
window
using a 80x24 grid.  Once I got that working, I added methods for
putting
characters in specific colors at specific locations.  Then parsing
ANSI.  Then
a lookup function with hueristics to figure out, 'hmm, HICOLOR CYAN
'D', what's
that?  Aha!  A silver dragon, or a baby silver.  My table says to put
up the adult
glyph in that case.  Hmm, a HICOLOR white '@'.  I have NO clue which
human
that is, so I'll leave it alone.'
Then linking in a socket object and learning enough telnet protocol to
be dangerous.
Then Alt commands.
Capturing different parts of the hi res screen and splitting into
message window, map window, and stat window.

I don't have enough experience with other windowing environments to
port it myself,
but to anyone willing, I can offer my insights as to *how* I did it.

